From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: MIRAGE <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 18:15:13 +0530
Subject: [PATCH] MIRAGE: the shell reads bash

Stock Cowrie's shell had ${NAME} and nothing else in braces, no [[ ]],
no (( )), and "command not found" for local, return, shift, eval, source,
declare, readonly and let. A script that uses any of them (most scripts)
failed on its first such line.

Syntax: every parameter expansion of bash 5.2 short of arrays (defaults,
prefix and suffix removal, replacement, case, substring, length,
indirection, ${!prefix*}, ${@:n}, ${v@Q}), brace expansion, $'...',
~user, NAME+=value, [[ ]] with patterns and regular expressions, (( )),
for ((;;)), "!" before a pipeline, and any compound command as a
function's body. [[ ]], (( )), for ((;;)) and "!" are rewritten into
commands with names no session can type (mirage_syntax).

Builtins: local, return, shift, let, declare / typeset with attributes,
readonly, export, unset, eval, source / ".", builtin, command, cd -,
pushd / popd / dirs, alias / unalias, hash, trap (EXIT fires), wait,
shopt, and set with -o.

What bash keeps for itself: set, declare -p, export -p and readonly
list bash's own variables with their attributes, in bash's two forms of
a value (mirage_vars); a function is printed back in bash's layout by
type, declare -f and set (mirage_print); a login shell has the
functions its /etc/profile.d defines.

Errors, as bash 5.2 gives them: a [[ ]] it cannot read is refused when
the line is read; an expansion it refuses, or an assignment to a
read-only variable, drops the rest of that line and no more; an error
inside a sourced file names the file, and inside a function defined in
a -c string says "environment"; "if then" and "do done" are syntax
errors; arithmetic errors use 5.2's words and keep the expression's
trailing blanks. "<<" inside $(( )) is a shift, not a here-document.

Background commands: "cmd &" ends the command there. Stock Cowrie gave
the "&" to the command as an argument ("echo hi &" printed "hi &"). The
job runs to its end where it stands; $!, wait PID, and a login shell's
"[1] PID" and "[1]+  Done" lines are bash's. sleep takes a fraction and
a unit.

mirage_shell_reference.jsonl holds 234 lines of bash recorded on the
reference host; the emulated shell answers 229 of them the same, and
the five that differ are named with their reason.
