+
+"""What bash does with ``${...}`` beyond a plain name, and two more things
+every script uses.
+
+Stock Cowrie's shell read ``${NAME}`` and nothing else in braces: ``${x:-y}``
+was a syntax error, and so was the whole line it stood on. These are in most
+scripts anyone pastes into a shell (defaults, stripping a suffix, the length
+of a string), so the line failed before a single command of it ran.
+
+:func:`expand` evaluates the text between ``${`` and ``}``. The words inside
+an expansion (a default, a pattern, a replacement) are themselves expanded by
+the caller's ``expand_word``, which knows whether the expansion stands in
+double quotes: there a single quote in a default is a character, outside it
+is quoting.
